WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.340
- Hi everyone, welcome
to our daily live stream.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.750
This is something that we
started about two months ago.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.170
Really to, stay in touch

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.130
during times of social distancing

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.000
and physical school closures.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.860
Obviously, we've been trying
to do a lot of other things,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.890
but we thought it's also nice
to have interesting guests

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.670
and have interesting conversations

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.330
about the crisis we're
going through and beyond.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.630
I do like to remind everyone

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.190
we are a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.300
We only exist because of
philanthropic donations

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.940
from folks like yourself.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.220
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.970
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.220
I wanna give special thanks
to several corporations

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.060
that have stepped up in the
recent last several months.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.710
We were already running at
a deficit before the crisis

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.540
and it grew as our server cost grew

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.110
and we've been trying to
accelerate some programs forward.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.650
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.600
AT&amp;T, fastly and Novartis.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.350
And, now we're gonna have a new segment

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:15.610
where I'm gonna bring in Dan,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.600
Hey, Dan.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.433
To just make a couple
- Hello.

00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:19.350
- of quick announcements for us.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.120
- All right, so we're super excited

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.240
to announce the launch of
our Spanish language U.S site

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.480
for remote learning,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.560
khan.co/sigamosaprendiendo

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.830
the folks, our team
will be adding the links

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.660
to the message boards right now.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.760
It's really exciting 'cause not only is it

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.170
for our U.S Spanish speaking folks,

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.060
it is also an adaptation of our resources.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.300
So, we didn't just translate,

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.450
we actually made sure
that we were adapting

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.780
all of our remote learning
resources appropriately.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.680
And then another announcement
we wanted to share

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.390
is that there will be no
a Homeroom show tomorrow,

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.100
Sal will be on, 'Hey There,
Human' with Rainn Wilson,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.830
better known as Dwight Schrute

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:02.020
on SoulPancake's Instagram
channel at noon tomorrow.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.250
But we will be back on Thursday

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.620
for a special Homeroom celebrating

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.750
the graduating class of 2020.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.220
So if you know any other
graduating seniors this year,

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.760
please, invite them to join Homeroom,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.470
Sal is gonna be sharing
some special messages

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.640
and the segments will be highlighting

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.860
all the great work that
you all did this past year.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.090
- Thanks, Dan, I'm mildly entertained

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.696
and disturbed by some of those
graphics that I just saw.

00:02:25.696 --> 00:02:27.859
- (laughs)

00:02:27.859 --> 00:02:28.830
- So thanks, Dan.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.150
And so I'm super excited
for our guest today,

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:36.040
Superintendent Alberto
Carvalho from Miami-Dade

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.040
who we've known each other
off and on for many years now.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.760
Superintendent Carvalho
thanks for joining us today.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.760
- It's a pleasure Sal,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.070
thank you so much for the opportunity,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.510
look forward to the conversation

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.690
and can't wait to see you
in Cap and Gown Thursday.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.520
- (laughs) My mum will
probably be excited,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.840
anytime it looks like I'm
getting more educated.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.880
So maybe good place to start before,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.020
obviously, it will be
interesting to talk about

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.310
how y'all have been navigating the crisis,

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.580
but what kinda blew my
mind was the first time

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.610
that we had a chance
to really dig in deeply

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.570
with everything going on at Miami-Dade.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:12.910
You know, there's a lot of narrative

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.920
of large urban school districts,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.280
you know, the stereotype is
that it can be a little bit

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.680
dysfunctional, a little bit slow moving.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.660
And I think, you know, I've
told this other people,

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:24.890
when you're not in the room,

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.583
something y'all have done at Miami-Dade

00:03:27.583 --> 00:03:29.190
it seems pretty incredible.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.510
Tell us kind of the
transformation of Miami-Dade

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.080
and where y'all have gotten with things

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.373
like graduation rates and other measures.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.260
- It's very interesting, we're
having that conversation now

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.610
in the middle of a crisis

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.280
because I'm a crisis Superintendent.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.480
I started black back in 2008
- 2009 as Superintendent

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.950
at the onset of one of
the greatest recessions

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.230
this nation ever went through.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.540
And quite frankly within that crisis,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.040
there was an opportunity
and then we'll go back to,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.560
how we're finding the opportunity wrapped

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.790
you know, in today's crisis.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.770
But you know, when I
started as Superintendent,

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:09.220
this was a district with
only 30% of students

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.280
were taking advantage of choice programs,

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.320
particularly advanced
academic choice programs,

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:16.860
graduation rate

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:17.960
- But what is the choice program?

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:19.010
Just for those of us.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.830
- At Tennis and Magnet
schools, single gender schools,

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:23.800
career academies,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.403
anything but a national
school environment.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:32.320
So graduation rates were
at 56% district wide,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.760
but behind that curtain
of general performance,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.290
there were some high schools
where the graduation rate

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.740
was as low as 35%.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.390
The shame of our community,

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:44.880
we had done nine schools
under a threat of shut down

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.420
by the state of Florida
because of performance.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.270
Because for as long as there
wasn't accountability system

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.000
these schools had never
posted anything higher,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.143
better than the D or an F.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.050
There were literally,
high schools in Miami-Dade

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.610
where zero students were taking advantage

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.530
of advanced placement
classes, IB or Cambridge.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.800
And that was reasonably disturbing.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.810
And of course on the natural setting,

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:17.000
I don't think Miami-Dade was
necessarily moving the needle.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.970
You fast forward and the decade later 73%

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.450
of our students are enrolled
in nontraditional programs.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.180
That means parents and students

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.510
are making choices about
the types of programs,

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:29.690
modality of learning,

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.730
something that actually excites them,

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:35.650
if they want robotics, coding,
performing visual arts,

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.960
cyber security, there is
something for everyone.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.470
A one size fits none in Miami-Dade,

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.450
over 1000 choice programs,

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:47.040
1000 parental options
for kids in Miami-Dade.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.510
Secondly, for the second year in a row

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.900
we are an A-rated school
system in the state of Florida,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.950
the highest level of performance.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:57.920
Three years in a row
with zero F rated schools

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.920
graduation rate this year at
90% from 56% and zero school

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.380
where the graduation rate below 80%.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.870
The school that used to
have a graduation rate,

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:15.290
at 35% is today at 80%.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.850
And the most remarkable thing
is during that time period

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.430
we became better you know,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.440
during times of crisis we had a chance

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.410
of reinventing ourselves
into a better version of us.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.480
Discarding a lot of the deadwood slang,

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.780
a lot of sacred cows that, you know,

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:34.623
anchor the status quo really well.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.850
Reinvented the school system
through digital convergence.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.870
That's why we were so prepared

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.870
for our transition to distance learning

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.870
because we passed a remarkable
bond referendum back in 2012

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:49.960
for $1.2 billion,

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.630
250 million of which
were spent in technology.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:55.570
And that was really
revolutionary in Miami-Dade

00:06:55.570 --> 00:07:00.570
from curating, developing,
acquiring applications, content,

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.090
digital content that's
personalized and adaptive

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.770
to ubiquitous universal WiFi guarantee,

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:09.760
expanded bandwidth across the board.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.740
And then the device conversation

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.570
we're not using bond referendums dollars

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.610
because I don't believe in
mortgaging for 30 years an asset

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.300
whose life span is two
and a half to three years,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.620
but we've acquired an
excess of 300,000 devices.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:29.080
And that's where we are today.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.540
You know, Broad winning district,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.090
one of the highest
performing urban districts

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.770
in the country with the highest percentage

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:38.690
of Cambridge passing
rate, AP passing rate.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.180
And when you look at who we are,

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:46.180
a minority district
95% minority, 75% poor,

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.100
50% of our student body is immigrant,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.520
80,000 of our kids are
English language learners.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.800
A lot of people would say, can't be done.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:57.930
But we've shown that the
impossible can become

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:01.233
the inevitable right here
in sunny South Florida.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:03.750
- And I wanna double click on that

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.360
before we even get to the present because

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.550
you know, I remember when
I first learned these,

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:09.930
for those of you who are listening,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:14.930
who are not familiar with
data from public schools

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.660
and large urban school districts,

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.780
what Superintendent
Carvalho just described

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:19.920
is not usual (laughing).

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:22.130
This is a major transformation

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.320
that a lot of people talk about.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.280
What in your mind, Superintendent Carvalho

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.780
I mean, even from your from
a leadership point of view

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:31.590
or what other stakeholders
are bringing on the table,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:32.650
what allowed you to do this?

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:36.210
Why can't we do this in
every city in the country

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.043
or in the world?

00:08:37.043 --> 00:08:38.120
Or what would have to happen?

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.830
Or what should the leaders be
doing to make that happened?

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.090
- Yeah, that's the
question I'm most asked.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:44.700
Everywhere I go, when I give a speech,

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:48.600
when I teach at Harvard,
everybody always asks me that.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.500
And, the answer that I give
is really fundamentally

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.360
based on five simple points.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.270
And the conclusion is that this is really

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.700
not that hard of work.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.630
It's no longer a skill set
that impairs or prevents

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:06.300
others from replicating these results.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.823
It's really a will set
proposition (laughing)

00:09:08.823 --> 00:09:10.840
- [Sal] Will set.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.390
- Yeah, they have the
political courage to actually

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:15.740
embrace the work

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.040
and the practice with a
degree of abandonment,

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:21.720
take risks and do right by kids

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.510
while sometimes irking the adults,

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.030
respecting adults, but
sometimes irking the adults.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:27.570
And certainly we've done that here.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.620
So the five things, number
one, leadership matters.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:33.953
And shortly after I became Superintendent,

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:37.660
principals are directly accountable to me.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.270
And I have to tell you that,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.310
and I don't say this with pride,

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:44.490
that I removed, replaced,
demoted, terminated,

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:48.240
promoted 85% of all principals

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:50.800
in the fourth largest
school system in America.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.610
And there's a reason for that.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.030
I was looking for a data-driven,

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:55.700
courageous instructional leaders.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:10:00.700
Who are a community minded
and are risk takers themselves

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.400
who can navigate complex data systems,

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.120
can identify gaps before they materialize

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.230
and develop strategies to address them.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.940
And that's the profile of
that leader that I want.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:13.290
Secondly, the reason why school leadership

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:16.580
is so important is because in Miami-Dade,

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.810
and I believe this is a good practice,

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:22.420
we screen teachers, but
principals hire teachers.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.250
So the hiring of teachers, you know,

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:26.940
the building block of
public education is done

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.160
at the school site.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.810
So I put a lot of trust in
my instructional leaders,

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:32.923
my captains who are principals.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.410
And that is the second critical element

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:41.410
is effective, strategic,
data-driven teaching

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.070
that is differentiated,

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:46.150
that utilizes all the assets we have,

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:49.860
that compliments the leadership style

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:50.930
of the principals we hired.

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:54.720
The third one, is in
fact digital convergence.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.720
And for me, this was our way
of tackling a huge inequity

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.240
that existed prior to our work here.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.020
Which by the way, America
came face to face with it,

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:08.890
during this pandemic crisis,
which is a digital inequity.

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:11.880
The fact that across the
country by Zip Code people

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.610
finally became aware of
what they already knew

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:16.930
is that there are a lot of digital deserts

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:21.680
and what digital deserts do,
to poor kids particularly

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.980
is it limits their ability to learn.

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:29.550
Their learning is limited
in that, bell to bell time

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:31.100
they have in school, why?

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.420
They don't have a device at home,

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:34.570
they don't have connectivity at home.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.520
So our digital convergence effort,

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.460
which began in 2012-2013,

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.640
empowered us with the ability

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.220
to actually create a portal of learning

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:47.300
for the most fragile kids,

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.410
by giving them a device,
personalized content,

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.100
applications and
connectivity if they need it.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.290
And by the way, developing
a parent Academy

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:59.543
to orient parents in assisting
their kids from home.

00:12:01.352 --> 00:12:03.530
That's number three.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:07.030
Number four, is parental engagement.

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:09.560
And by the way, I leave
it for number four,

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:12.110
because I can't change the parent

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.050
as much as I can change the child,

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:16.190
but I can influence the parent.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:19.370
And our launch of the Parent Academy

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:20.970
as a college for parents,

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.940
number one, they help
parents help their own kids,

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:25.230
navigate schooling.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.040
Second, empower parents with skill sets,

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:29.640
to get them a job,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.700
in our own school system,
in our technical colleges.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:35.390
And thirdly, empower parents
in an immigrant community

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.020
to be civic minded.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:38.780
Know their rights, know the laws,

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:41.853
take advantage of the protections
that are afforded them.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:46.940
But, the real reason
for the Parent Academy

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:49.470
and this hurts the
feelings of some educators

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:53.610
out there in America, is I
want to make parents dangerous.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:56.950
You know, just like the law of
economics, supply and demand,

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:01.560
you know we all know communities
that are demand sided

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.410
in terms their parents,
others are supply sided.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:07.090
Demand side communities
it's those communities,

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.260
reasonably affluent, parents know exactly

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.460
where the best school, the best
program, the best principal,

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:15.130
the best teacher, and they
demand that their kid be in it.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.720
Then you have certain
communities where parents

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:19.960
unfortunately don't really
know what to ask for.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:22.100
They supply with their
children with a hope and prayer

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.220
and wish that you do
something right by them.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.530
So part of my theory of action here is,

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:29.080
I want to feel pressure from communities

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:33.300
that have not necessarily
express their discontent,

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:36.060
sometimes with what happens
with our children's school

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.680
that's empowering parents.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.580
And then the last element

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.790
is effective, targeted, strategic
human capital development,

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:46.910
professional development,
really retooling, re-skilling

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:48.520
the workforce that we have.

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:49.820
Look, you cannot go out there

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:50.970
and trade your army.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:53.120
By the way, my army is fantastic.

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.680
So it's hiring the right people,

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:59.680
but it's also accelerating
the pace that you transition,

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.870
the army of employees you have,

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:05.690
particularly teachers and support staff

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:08.720
into a new way of thinking,
a new way of teaching,

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.200
a new way of supporting,
a new way of using data,

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.140
new way of using technology.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.270
You do those five, and I
guarantee you any school system,

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.153
rural, urban, small, large, will prosper.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:21.583
- Now, those are great.

00:14:21.583 --> 00:14:23.350
And I'm getting a lot of questions

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:24.590
showing up on YouTube and Facebook.

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:25.490
Before I go into that,

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:27.110
I actually have a leadership question.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.880
And also, before we
talk about COVID crisis,

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.560
you know, you talking
about those five things

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.180
and they make a ton of sense.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:37.030
But I love your phrase the will set,

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:39.080
the willpower to do it, you know,

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:42.900
to go in as a new Superintendent and to,

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:44.500
you know, make some of these changes,

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:46.370
some of them quite dramatic.

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:48.320
It's easier said than done.
- Yeah

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:50.140
- I'm curious, at a personal level,

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:50.973
what went through your head?

00:14:50.973 --> 00:14:52.040
At sometimes we say,

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:55.410
Hey Alberto, maybe I'm the wrong one.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.780
Maybe I shouldn't rock the boat too much.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:59.380
Maybe I should just kinda play nice.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:00.260
I mean how do you,

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:01.870
'cause I weigh those things all the time.

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:03.540
Sometimes I feel
conviction about something,

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.480
but I'm the only person who believes them

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.130
like maybe I should just
back off a little bit.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.840
- (Laughs) I'll tell
you a funny story, Sal.

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.180
Number one, I do believe in
working at the edge of the abyss

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:18.100
because if you don't know how deep the gap

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.024
where our kids live is

00:15:20.024 --> 00:15:22.660
and you don't know until
you get really close to it

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:24.420
and you don't get really close to it

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.370
unless you take immense risks.

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.470
So when I talk about abandonment,

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:29.730
yeah, I'm talking about abandonment

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.410
for the position that I
hold, I risk the position.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:36.570
I try very hard, I tell
this to people all the time

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:37.760
to get fired.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:39.420
And it is in that precious moment

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.120
where you flirt with termination

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.330
that's something big
happens for the district.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:45.780
If it's big enough for you
to make a personal burden,

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.810
trust me things will happen.

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:49.710
And that happened quite frankly,

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.650
within two weeks of being
appointed Superintendent

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:53.210
goes directly to your question.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:54.680
Look, remember the nine schools,

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:55.600
they were gonna be shut down

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:57.060
by the state of Florida for performance.

00:15:57.060 --> 00:16:00.110
And by the way, these were
all black and brown schools

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:01.170
in high poverty areas.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.320
If you shut down the
school for performance,

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:05.060
the only thing that will be on that street

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:08.190
will be a liquor store upon
a shop may be a church.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:09.560
And the beacon of hope and opportunity

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:11.580
for that community is extinguished.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.810
I was not going to do that, Okay.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:16.150
There was a greater chance
of the crown of Spain

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:19.300
of reclaiming Florida than
me giving up on the schools.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:22.820
So I brought the nine
principles of the schools

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:25.110
one at a time to have
a conversation with me.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:27.120
And I had a conversation with them,

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:28.370
ask them all the questions.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:31.680
They had been there for
a long period of time.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:34.830
And the one question they
all across the board failed

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:35.700
was when I asked them,

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:39.060
why don't you have a single AP class,

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:42.190
AP literature, AP physics,
chemistry, give me something,

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:43.313
AP Spanish.

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:45.960
And across the board the answer was,

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:48.850
you know, I'd love to, but
I don't have kids for that.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:50.380
At that point, I didn't
have a job for them.

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:52.310
I fired nine principals.

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:56.660
Well, nobody had told me that
over the previous 90 years,

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:00.600
the school system had not
fired nine principals.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.130
And I remember the board
chair at that time,

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:04.890
taking me aside and said,

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.077
and saying to me two weeks in,

00:17:07.077 --> 00:17:10.897
"You know, I regret voting
for you as Superintendent.

00:17:10.897 --> 00:17:12.447
"You fired my friends."

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:18.520
Now it burnt, it was
rough, but I survived.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.340
And once you go through
one of those experiences,

00:17:21.340 --> 00:17:22.430
you build resiliency.

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:25.450
When you time that, with
a window of opportunity,

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:28.380
and by the way, those don't
just come once in a lifetime.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.790
They happen, they happen sickly.

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:33.810
We have one happening right
now, a window of opportunity,

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:37.203
then you can get really
crazy good things done.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.920
Not devoid of threat or challenge.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:44.560
But if you're grounded
in that, which is right,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.490
then you take on the sacred cows,

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.310
you take on the political establishment

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.060
and you force through
that, which you know,

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:55.740
will actually pay off for kids.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:59.910
Do I worry sometimes,
I myself not as much.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.830
Sometimes my team does,

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:04.280
sometimes my wife may a bit.

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:07.460
But I feel the community is on our side

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:08.400
we're very transparent.

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:09.923
I tell the story all the time.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.620
The media believe it or not, 12 years in,

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:17.020
I don't complain about the
media, the print media, TV media

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:22.020
because they recognize that
we live it all out there.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.440
So yeah, there are some tough times.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.470
But there are enough
people in this community

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:30.620
who are awake and support the work

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:32.870
and provide a cover for
the work to continue.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:34.789
- That's super powerful.

00:18:34.789 --> 00:18:35.770
Just, you know, you do it right

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:37.270
and the chips fall where they do

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:40.260
even for yourself versus you know,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.990
preserving a job or title
or whatever's expedient.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:45.440
I think it's a very powerful notion.

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.080
- A lot of folks out there do what I do

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:51.580
that never really risked
that much out of fear

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:53.730
of pushing the envelope too far.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:54.563
And you know, what happens?

00:18:54.563 --> 00:18:55.970
They end up losing the job

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.550
because the performance never increases.

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:01.210
And so at least, you know,

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:04.930
lose your job doing something
big, bold and right,

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:06.100
that actually moves the needle

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:07.290
because you have greater chances

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.940
of actually keeping your
job if the needle moves

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:11.700
because people pay attention.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.560
So you know, that to me
it's my theory of action

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:15.743
and it's worked.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:21.230
I recommend it strongly
to those who you know,

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:24.020
who don't mind you know,
feeling the pressure

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:26.620
that comes with a territory,
but it's worked for us.

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:28.660
- Alright, that's fascinating.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:30.120
So I'm gonna go into
some of these questions

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.840
and really get more to the present.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:34.757
You know, Susanna Garcia
Domingez on YouTube is asking,

00:19:34.757 --> 00:19:37.007
"Greetings Superintended Carvalho

00:19:37.007 --> 00:19:39.433
"what are your takeaways
from this pandemic crisis?"

00:19:39.433 --> 00:19:42.610
We have another one from,

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:44.080
I just thought it just disappeared

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:45.730
where a student was asking about

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:47.260
just how you navigating the crisis?

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:49.630
So yet play it out for us.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:51.650
You know, how did this all evolve in Miami

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:54.230
over the last I guess
two and a half months

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:55.230
when it started to become clear

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.330
that we might have to have to close?

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:00.140
- Yeah, so you may find this incredible,

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:02.810
but we did not wait on
the federal government

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:06.230
or state of Florida to tell
us to provide us directives

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:09.130
on how to prepare for what was inevitable.

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:13.710
Actually I remember us back in January,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:18.710
simultaneously we were
digesting headlines out of China

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:23.060
and I had just watched a series on Netflix

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:28.060
dealing with a pandemic
and I connected the two.

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:33.980
A week later coronavirus
is on my leadership agenda

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:35.580
to be discussed with my cabinet.

00:20:36.687 --> 00:20:41.420
And a few weeks later, we were
rating a survey for parents

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:44.180
to determine what the critical technology

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:46.920
needs were at home, why?

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:49.990
Because we put on the board
the worst case scenario

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:51.090
to solve for.

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.660
And that worst case scenario is,

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.000
all schools gonna shut down,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:59.140
355,000 K12 kids gonna be home

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.310
in addition to 40,000 adult learners,

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:04.963
48,000 employees will be home.

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:06.860
What you gonna do?

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:08.080
What's our solution for that?

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:11.140
So we bet on the worst case scenario,

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:11.973
- And when was it?

00:21:11.973 --> 00:21:14.298
Was this in February or January?

00:21:14.298 --> 00:21:16.040
- January process...

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:17.940
- January, you having this
conversations in January

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.620
no one was talking about school
closures back in January.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:20.892
- No one, no.
- Well

00:21:20.892 --> 00:21:24.020
- We began a contingency plan in January.

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:27.180
February we began to
actually write the plan

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:31.460
two weeks before the shutdown.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:32.690
We have the first version

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:34.830
of our instructional continuity plan,

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:38.750
which was followed by
an ICP 2.0 and much more

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:41.760
evolved with student and
teacher accountability.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:44.973
Look, we shut down schools with an MOU

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:51.023
and negotiated agreement
with the teacher's union,

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:54.360
agreeing to accountability,

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:57.760
you know, number of hours
that we're expecting

00:21:57.760 --> 00:21:59.630
in terms of responsiveness to students

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:01.850
and connectivity in connection.

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:04.010
Same thing, attendance for students.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.640
We didn't have to do this on the backend.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:07.390
Before we shut down schools,

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:10.790
we had already developed
Webinars for teachers.

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:13.480
We had already put 100% of our teachers

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.010
through professional development,

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:17.450
which was not difficult
because they were already using

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:18.580
the same applications,

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:20.520
the same devices in the classroom

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:23.460
that now the, kids and
the teachers themselves

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:24.820
were using at home.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:28.343
The geography changed,
but nothing else did.

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:32.020
The survey results empowered
us with the ability

00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:33.760
to actually know exactly

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:36.700
who needed a device, who needed a hotspot.

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:40.570
So when we began the
distribution, 119,000 devices

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:42.160
hit the hands of kids quickly,

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:46.310
11,000 hotspots, 100%
connectivity in Miami-Dade.

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:49.170
And by the way, and
this is still right now,

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:54.170
average daily attendance
hold on America 91 to 92%.

00:22:55.740 --> 00:23:00.740
That's about one to 2% away
from a typical day last year

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:02.403
around this time.

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:05.500
Because we prepared,

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:07.630
we were not waiting on
the federal government

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:09.550
to say it's time to go now.

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:11.620
We anticipated worst case scenario

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:14.720
and we prepared for it
hoping for the best.

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:17.280
And that's exactly what we're
doing right now by the way,

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:20.910
for the reopening, come August 20.

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:23.230
We are, you know, these days

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:27.740
it's less about strategic planning.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:31.330
It's more about active strategic thinking

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:35.450
that is nimble, that is
swift, that can pivot

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:39.410
on the basis of environmental
changes in our communities.

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:42.900
And I think leadership
today is having a number

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.800
of contingency plans ready jump to,

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:49.090
depending on conditions
as they emerge between now

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:50.860
and the reopening of schools,

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:53.600
however, that happens in August.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:55.500
And that's exactly what we're doing.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:58.820
We were not waiting from the landlord

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:03.080
to tell us when to rearrange
the furniture at our level.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:04.180
We got ready for that.

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:06.283
- And there's several things

00:24:06.283 --> 00:24:07.530
I mean, we could double click

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:08.710
on a lot of what you just said,

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:11.660
but I'm curious how you
were able to, so quickly,

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:13.100
I mean it sounds like
one of your advantages,

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:15.470
you saw it coming and you planned ahead,

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:17.560
but you know, just the
devices that you mentioned,

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.360
a hundred something thousand devices,

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:20.370
a large chunk of the students.

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:22.190
I mean, it sounds like
you've kind of closed

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.190
the digital divide in your community

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:27.180
somewhat you know, not
overnight but over a few weeks.

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:29.893
How did you pull that off?

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:32.860
- Data, data, data.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:36.733
You know, data is part of
our DNA, is our North Star.

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:40.010
We have an equity mindset.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:42.140
We knew exactly where the opportunity gaps

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:45.660
were for kids and we planned to address it

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:49.090
in a very strategic,
methodical, procedural way.

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:54.090
And you're right, as far as
the data that we manage daily

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:57.020
and every single day at 9:00 a.m

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.940
I get the reports from my senior team

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:03.790
before we start today
via Zoom conversation

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:06.000
that looks at system wide performance.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.280
But then looks at disaggregated data

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.280
for the lowest 25% of students

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:13.120
in terms of reading proficiency

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:14.490
for English language learners,

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:16.360
for students with disabilities,

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.053
for the immigrant child.

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:22.380
I get all of those reports
in addition to attendance

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:24.070
for every one of these groups.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:27.630
So we can very quickly address

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:31.040
the pockets of low
performance or the outliers.

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:32.920
I mean, there was a point
where we had students

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.650
who were unaccounted for.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.110
Guess what?

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:37.720
We used wellness checks,

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:39.300
even our own police department,

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:40.250
with a knock on the door,

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:45.250
a social worker, a device in
the bag with easy instructions.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.020
We leveraged the fact that
we continue to feed kids

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:52.080
and again, buckle your
seat belt once again.

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.610
As of today, we fed

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:58.610
2.9 million meals out of 50 centers,

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.050
we do it twice a week for every single day

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:03.840
for every single kid.

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:06.680
No questions asked that
comes to our schools.

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:07.640
But in addition to that,

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:09.080
because we know that some kids live

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:12.640
in really harsh conditions
facing a food insecurity

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.020
in their Zip Codes,

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:17.620
we embrace and embarked on
this community feeding project,

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:19.570
not using taxpayer's money,

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:22.940
but through our foundation
capturing money,

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:25.130
then putting small restaurants to work,

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:28.320
to prepare meals to
distribute to the Zip Codes

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:30.780
where we had the highest poverty

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:33.430
overlaid over this grid that demonstrated

00:26:33.430 --> 00:26:36.870
the largest concentration of poor kids.

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:41.870
So have we filled or
narrowed some of the gaps?

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.280
In some cases, we obliterated the gap.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.410
In many cases, it's work in progress,

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.000
but it's what we're
doing now quite frankly,

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:54.180
as we wrap up their school year

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.300
and the end of next school year

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:58.640
that I'm excited and if we have a chance

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.400
I'd like to talk about it.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:02.440
- Yeah now, we got a bunch of questions

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:04.346
there from YouTube, Charlotte,

00:27:04.346 --> 00:27:05.847
"What is the plan for next school year?

00:27:05.847 --> 00:27:08.100
"Will it be digital or
will it be mixed learning?"

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:09.607
Kim darling from YouTube,

00:27:09.607 --> 00:27:11.580
"Do you anticipate going back to school?"

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:13.340
Or yeah, so speak to that.
- Yeah.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:16.200
- What are the contingency
plans you're thinking of?

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:18.850
- So number one, I have
absolute expectation,

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:20.020
I'm the eternal optimist.

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:22.570
Kids will go back to schooling,

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:24.440
Now going back to school and when?

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:27.500
That's a function, since
I'm a science driven guy,

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:31.830
you know, my degree is
in Biomedical Sciences

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:32.890
with a minor in Physics.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:37.520
I have no choice, It's in
my DNA to decode science

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:40.070
and use it to inform the practice.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:41.870
So what I can tell you right now is,

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:48.670
what we're doing really in a
very proud and deliberate way

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:50.720
is dealing with,

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:53.723
America will experience
very soon which is,

00:27:55.260 --> 00:28:00.080
the largest precedent setting
historic academic regression,

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:02.180
the likes of which the
nation has never seen.

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:05.410
And by the way, it's going
to impact disproportionally

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:08.840
the kids who are always at
the bottom to begin with.

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:11.593
These are, by the way, the
kids who before the crisis

00:28:11.593 --> 00:28:13.680
were already in crisis,

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:14.810
which is interesting, right?

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:16.300
There were a lot of kids amongst us,

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:19.770
before COVID-19 crisis they
were already experiencing

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:24.120
a multifaceted crisis on a daily basis.

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:27.990
So for us, this is the
educational opportunity hidden

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:29.680
in this health crisis.

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:33.000
And it begins with two summer sessions.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:36.660
And by the way, all of our
budget's gonna be hit hard.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:41.030
But this is my priority,
budget before my board.

00:28:41.030 --> 00:28:41.910
Two summer sessions.

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:44.460
The very first one is a virtual session

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:46.780
targeting a student's already identified

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:49.970
on a basis of performance,
on the basis of fragility,

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:53.970
on the basis of their
lackluster connection

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:55.660
during distance learning

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:58.163
on the basis of formative assessment data.

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:02.130
The second summer session may be virtual

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:04.970
or should conditions
improve significantly,

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:08.900
we may bring students into
schools in small groups,

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:13.790
small ratios for very individualized
attention by teachers,

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:16.460
credit recovery, students
with disabilities,

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:18.370
immigrant students and the like.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:21.840
Then we already budgeted
for and planned for,

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.410
the next school year to
start two weeks earlier

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:28.150
for 25% of our student population.

00:29:28.150 --> 00:29:32.380
That's 46,000 students opening all schools

00:29:32.380 --> 00:29:35.720
taught by selected teachers

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:39.420
who have a high record of success

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:41.470
with these minority populations,

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:43.340
with these struggling students.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:47.720
Next, virtual mentors and
tutors for every single kid.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:50.000
Next for 25 schools

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.740
that have the greatest percentage
of these fragile children,

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:54.870
one hour added at the end of the day,

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:58.500
for additional opportunity to catch up.

00:29:58.500 --> 00:30:01.500
What I'm describing here
is, the opportunity for us

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.010
is to academically stabilize these kids

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:06.160
who historically have fallen behind

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:10.000
by the end of the 2020-2021 school year.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:14.270
Now what will school look like next year?

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:16.010
Guess what America,

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:18.180
since distance learning became a reality

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:20.670
across all 50 states,

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:23.250
parents have had a unique opportunity

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:27.560
to have a front row seat
and observe the whole show.

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:29.320
They have been monitoring what's happening

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:30.920
in their kids' education.

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:35.290
They have made judgments,
evaluated the experience,

00:30:35.290 --> 00:30:37.080
they've determined how responsive

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:39.180
the teacher was or wasn't.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:42.920
They know the level of
engagement of their own children.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:45.270
And I think parents as a result of this,

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:48.710
are not going to give up that knowledge.

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:50.130
So that's why this new normal,

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:52.960
everybody keeps talking about it's real.

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:54.780
Parents will be more demanding

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:56.410
and I think there'll be more demanding

00:30:56.410 --> 00:30:59.250
in terms of expectations
for their children.

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:03.320
But now that they know
that it is possible,

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.680
my prediction is, and this
is what we're planning for

00:31:06.680 --> 00:31:07.770
in Miami-Dade.

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.000
Again one size fits none,

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:14.240
is a very flexible opportunity
to schedule students

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:18.300
and teach students on the
basis of individual student

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:22.423
modality, flexibility and
demand on a parent's life,

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:26.350
the logistics that will
condition our actions,

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:28.950
social distancing,
measuring of temperature

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:30.700
kids going into schools,

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:35.320
having to reconfigure schools
the way for example, you feed

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:37.460
probably you're not gonna
be able to congregate kids

00:31:37.460 --> 00:31:38.600
in a cafeteria.

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:41.470
So food will be distributed
to each classroom

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:43.373
like Uber Eats to the classroom.

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:48.280
Unidirectional hallways to reduce contact,

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:51.040
cyclical mandatory washing of hands,

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:55.890
much stronger systems for
illness detection and reporting.

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:58.430
But what I'm excited about quite frankly

00:31:58.430 --> 00:32:01.480
is the fact that we
will see in my opinion,

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:05.280
an explosion of hybrid than
blended learning models.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.970
And I'll just gave you an
example, three examples.

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:10.830
Little Johnny because of
parental circumstances

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:13.560
or the fact that he is
a highly social being

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:16.010
wants to be in school 100% of the time.

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:18.880
Our school system ought to
be able to deliver that.

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:21.890
Mary on the other hand
may say, you know what,

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:25.160
my parents, one parent is
at home part of the time

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:27.900
and I'd like to be home part of the time.

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:31.690
So I would like to be
on Mondays, Wednesdays

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:32.980
and Fridays at home.

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:35.300
On Tuesdays and Thursdays
actually at school.

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:37.490
We can do that too, why not?

00:32:37.490 --> 00:32:39.810
Then there may be yet another child

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:43.430
who for a whole host of reasons may say,

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:46.070
I actually thrive in this environment

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:48.600
and I want to make school visitations.

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:50.830
I want to take advantage
of some of the programs,

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:52.070
athletics, et cetera.

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:57.070
But I feel safer at least
for the beginning of the year

00:32:57.500 --> 00:32:59.320
based on parental concerns,

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:02.620
staying home and learning from a distance.

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:03.680
What I just described,

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:06.000
this sort of a spectrum of opportunity

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:07.750
that is choice driven,

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:10.270
that identifies the needs
of parents and students

00:33:10.270 --> 00:33:12.240
and tries to match them

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:15.560
with individualized learning pathways

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:17.760
they are suited and suitable for each kid.

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:20.120
And I think that's
what's going to explode.

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:22.720
It's sort of the new dimension
of choice in America.

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:24.510
- That's super powerful.

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:27.640
'Cause if you take it one
intellectual step from there,

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:29.570
that means that even
the physical experiences

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:31.620
will have to support the
virtual and that to some degree

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:32.850
you're not necessarily bound

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:35.283
to one physical location
anymore, in theory.

00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:38.360
- That is absolutely right.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:42.670
And look, I'm already writing
letters and advocating

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:44.580
based on the experience we've had,

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:47.440
I think state houses and
the federal government

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:49.220
needs to rethink a number of things.

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:51.700
Number one, how education is funded.

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:54.290
I mean I think we're proving that,

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:56.290
having everybody start at the same time

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:59.710
and at the same time for the
exact same number of minutes

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:04.590
every single day in the same
place, doesn't work anymore.

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:07.250
So we need to liberalize
funding methodology

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:09.360
for public education across the country,

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:14.010
so that mandated seat time
is not a mandatory feature

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:16.650
in student funding.

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:21.650
Secondly, the how, when,
by whom and how often

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:25.810
something is taught and what is taught

00:34:25.810 --> 00:34:27.780
needs to be looked at as well.

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:31.480
And, I think that what we
have learned under duress,

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:33.090
right in the rush,

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:37.160
can now over the summer and
in months to come be analyzed.

00:34:37.160 --> 00:34:41.100
And let's use some of the
learnings from this experience

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:44.810
to actually enhance the student
experience moving forward.

00:34:44.810 --> 00:34:46.760
And I think that's reasonably powerful.

00:34:47.700 --> 00:34:49.720
- Wow well you know,
Superintendent Carvalho

00:34:49.720 --> 00:34:51.080
I could talk to you for a few more hours.

00:34:51.080 --> 00:34:52.750
Actually wanna take notes (laughs)

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:54.580
on a lot of this because
what you're doing,

00:34:54.580 --> 00:34:58.660
I mean, at a very large scale
with a complex school district

00:34:58.660 --> 00:34:59.830
and then there's a lot more houses,

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:00.663
there's a lot of questions.

00:35:00.663 --> 00:35:02.050
How are you able to pull this off?

00:35:02.050 --> 00:35:03.280
You know, what you're saying

00:35:03.280 --> 00:35:04.770
makes so much intellectual sense,

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:08.190
but you know the timeframes
and obviously the dollars,

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:10.660
where are you getting
the resources to do it?

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:13.130
You know, I guess you're
willing to stake your job

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:13.963
on a lot of things.

00:35:13.963 --> 00:35:16.480
We talked about that, so that's the will.

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:17.720
I mean, I actually just answer

00:35:17.720 --> 00:35:18.800
if you have a couple more minutes.

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:22.750
I'd love, you know, how
are you able to do this?

00:35:22.750 --> 00:35:24.080
How are you able to do these programs?

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:25.580
Where are you finding the resources?

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:26.660
You talked a lot of places

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:28.080
like, Oh, we don't have an extra penny,

00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:31.110
we're already, you know, over
our heads in terms of budget.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:33.490
- Yeah, well you know I
think real number one,

00:35:33.490 --> 00:35:36.150
let's recognize that the fiscal shock

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:38.160
that our nation is about to experience,

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:40.830
the fiscal shock that a
public educational systems

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:43.550
across America are about to face

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:45.250
is gonna be a daunting one.

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:47.930
But we've been there before,
probably not to this degree.

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:52.280
So, you know, I don't wanna
give a Tallahassee my case

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:55.960
or Washington DC a pass.

00:35:55.960 --> 00:35:57.860
During the last economic recession

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:02.090
under stimulus packages back then,

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:06.420
over $115 billion were
appropriated to stabilize

00:36:06.420 --> 00:36:08.370
educational opportunities
across the country.

00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:10.670
So far under the CARES act,

00:36:10.670 --> 00:36:12.800
the current federal stimulus packages

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:15.310
only $16 billion have been earmarked.

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:17.765
And trust me by all accounts, you know,

00:36:17.765 --> 00:36:22.440
and we read Moody's analytics
as often as we possibly can.

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:26.390
The prediction is that what's
coming is a more powerful,

00:36:26.390 --> 00:36:29.020
longer-lasting economic recession

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:32.010
than the great recession of 2008-2009.

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:35.020
So the federal government
needs to do quite a bit more.

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:39.620
At the same time, I'm
one who believes that,

00:36:39.620 --> 00:36:43.960
reductions across the board
don't require leadership.

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:46.450
So even during tough times,

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:49.130
you ought to prioritize that
which is most important.

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:50.620
And that's where we start.

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:52.850
We follow something we
branded here in Miami,

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:56.140
which is values based,
zero based budgeting.

00:36:56.140 --> 00:37:00.560
Each year, starts at zero
with the highest priorities

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:03.330
being funded first, everything
else is second there.

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:05.130
And that's exactly what we're doing.

00:37:06.170 --> 00:37:09.640
We are preparing for this fiscal shock.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:11.817
So we turned off the, you know,

00:37:11.817 --> 00:37:16.400
the spending rivers long ago,

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:19.800
non essential hiring
stopped, procurement stopped,

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:22.720
renegotiation of private sector contracts

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:26.150
at a discount 10% minimally.

00:37:26.150 --> 00:37:30.300
Because whatever the
cost was for something,

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:33.300
a service or a good three months ago,

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.070
it's not the same today.

00:37:35.070 --> 00:37:38.240
You know as a nation, we
went from 3% unemployment

00:37:38.240 --> 00:37:40.480
to over 20% in six weeks.

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:43.800
Everything has changed, which
means we need to change.

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:46.720
So we will face the fiscal crisis

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:50.170
we have and have amassed
reasonable reserves.

00:37:50.170 --> 00:37:53.660
We passed two bond
referendum that have secured

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:57.340
teacher salaries in a
very significant way.

00:37:57.340 --> 00:37:59.870
We boosted our teacher salaries last year,

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:03.360
anywhere from 14 to 23%
- Wow!

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:04.810
- and improve security.

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:07.350
And that's not in danger because

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:11.650
the funding stream for
that is property tax based,

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:13.190
not state revenue.

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:14.680
So that's gonna remain stable.

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:17.090
Our investment in technology
is gonna remain stable.

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:19.650
Our upkeep of our school
is gonna remain stable,

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:22.523
because we saw the need to help ourselves.

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:27.050
Operations, yes we will depend
on state and federal revenues

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:28.570
and we're working with those as well.

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:29.870
But there's always an opportunity

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:32.860
to prioritize that which
is important in a budget.

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:35.050
We don't believe in 5% across the board,

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:36.530
10% across the board.

00:38:36.530 --> 00:38:38.540
In fact, during times of recession,

00:38:38.540 --> 00:38:41.840
it is customary for us to
actually augment funding

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:43.620
for certain critical priorities,

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:47.000
like what we're doing for the
fragile student population

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:49.370
rather than shying away from it.

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:51.750
Obviously other areas will have to be cut,

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:55.046
but those are judgment calls
on basis of priorities.

00:38:55.046 --> 00:38:57.920
And that's what leaders
right now in America

00:38:57.920 --> 00:38:58.860
should be doing.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:00.920
You know what's most important?

00:39:00.920 --> 00:39:02.560
What are the priorities?

00:39:02.560 --> 00:39:06.810
What are the gaps, that if you
don't address them right now

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:11.240
will grow dramatically between
now and August of 2020,

00:39:11.240 --> 00:39:15.070
making it more expensive for you to engage

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:18.750
in the remediation, which I
don't believe in remediation.

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:22.330
I believe in accelerating
towards every child's potential.

00:39:22.330 --> 00:39:23.760
But those are the considerations

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:24.820
and that's how we're doing it.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:26.870
We're navigating the same waters,

00:39:26.870 --> 00:39:30.330
but we have a different
sail, a different rudder

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:32.630
and we're reading the
stars in different ways.

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:36.210
- Wow, well that's super inspiring.

00:39:36.210 --> 00:39:38.400
And you know, I think as
we go through tough times

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:39.500
whether it's in a school system,

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:40.690
you're a superintendent out there

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:42.400
or you're just a leader in anything.

00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:44.890
I think there's been a lot
of really powerful takeaways.

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:47.290
So thank you so much,
Superintendent Carvalho.

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:49.650
I hope we can continue this conversation

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:50.860
especially over the next few months.

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:52.900
I would love to learn
how all of this evolves,

00:39:52.900 --> 00:39:54.720
and I think you're really
on the cutting edge

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:56.220
of thinking through how
this is going to play out.

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:57.480
Thank you so much.

00:39:57.480 --> 00:39:58.313
- So I really appreciate it,

00:39:58.313 --> 00:40:00.060
you being a terrific partner.

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:01.860
Let's continue to do what we do.

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:04.320
Not only with Khan Academy,

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:07.600
but with the College Board the
Council of Great City Schools

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:09.963
and to all the educators out there listen,

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:15.560
schooling will resume and that
is important for us to be,

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:19.730
a steady stream of normalcy for kids.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:21.210
Not only intellectually, academically

00:40:21.210 --> 00:40:23.240
but also socially and emotionally.

00:40:23.240 --> 00:40:25.030
And if you cannot do a face to face,

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:27.560
there are means by which
today using technology,

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:28.720
you can do it from a distance.

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:29.830
And that's what we've been doing.

00:40:29.830 --> 00:40:32.503
So good luck to all of
you and talk to you soon.

00:40:33.490 --> 00:40:35.537
- Thank you so much.
- Thank you.

00:40:36.730 --> 00:40:37.990
- Well thank you everyone for joining,

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:40.410
hopefully you're as inspired as I was,

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:42.170
by talking to Superintendent Carvalho.

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:44.170
I think, not just a lessons
on where we're going

00:40:44.170 --> 00:40:47.390
on the education front but
frankly, leadership lessons

00:40:47.390 --> 00:40:49.250
I thought were pretty profound.

00:40:49.250 --> 00:40:50.140
So thank you so much.

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:52.660
And as Dan mentioned, we're
not gonna be there tomorrow.

00:40:52.660 --> 00:40:55.820
I'm gonna, I've obviously
been a big office fan,

00:40:55.820 --> 00:40:57.950
so I'm excited to meet Rainn Wilson

00:40:57.950 --> 00:41:00.380
or also known as Dwight Schrute tomorrow.

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:01.470
So that should be entertaining.

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:04.800
And then we'll resume
backup I think on Thursday.

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:06.970
But either way everyone
have a good evening

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:09.707
and I'll see you one way or
the other tomorrow (laughs).

